1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,320
Today on the show, I sat down with SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce.

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:07,640
Listeners of the show will know that we have long been big fans of Commissioner Peirce

3
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:12,560
and her proactive work to promote pro-innovation policies in the blockchain sector.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:17,100
In this episode, we discussed the past, the present, and the future of the SEC as it relates

5
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:18,760
to blockchain technology.

6
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:23,000
We talked about the role of Congress and the path forward, and we talked about some tangible

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,080
advice for founders in the digital asset ecosystem.

8
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:27,580
I think you'll enjoy this one.

9
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:33,180
So without further ado, here's my conversation with SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce.

10
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:35,540
Matt Walsh and Nick Carter are partners at Castle Island Ventures.

11
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:38,180
All of these expressed by them or the guests on this podcast are solely their opinions

12
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:40,740
and do not reflect the opinions of Castle Island Ventures.

13
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:42,860
Guests and hosts may maintain positions in the assets discussed in this podcast.

14
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:46,420
You should not treat any opinion expressed by anyone on this podcast as a specific inducement

15
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:50,100
to make a particular investment or follow a particular strategy, but only as an expression

16
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,100
of their personal opinion.

17
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:54,220
This podcast is for informational purposes only.

18
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:59,300
Cut down by bad mortgage investments, Lehman, which has 25,000 employees, will be liquidated.

19
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:03,980
The federal government loans American International Group, AIG, 85 billion dollars.

20
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:06,820
This is a different kind of market and the Fed is asleep!

21
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:11,020
The federal government is stepping in to stabilize Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, the two mortgage

22
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:13,500
giants that have been threatened by the housing crisis.

23
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:17,340
The Bank of England has pumped 75 billion pounds more into Britain's ailing economy

24
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:18,780
with a new round of quantitative easing.

25
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:22,180
You print a couple trillion dollars and all of a sudden people start to worry.

26
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:27,100
So out of this worry, we have something called the Bitcoin, Bitcoin.

27
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,300
Commissioner Peirce, thank you so much for coming on the podcast.

28
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:31,020
I feel like this has been a long time coming.

29
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:32,700
We've wanted to have you on for quite a while.

30
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:34,900
Well, Matt, it's a pleasure to be here.

31
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:36,900
I am a big follower of your show.

32
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:38,420
I love to listen to it.

33
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:41,740
I of course have to give you my disclaimer, which is that my views are my own views as

34
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:45,260
a commissioner, not necessarily those of the SEC or my fellow commissioners.

35
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:46,260
Awesome.

36
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:49,060
I'm used to hearing that disclaimer and no surprise.

37
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:53,660
I'd love to just start this maybe with a personal question of what has it been like for you

38
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:56,020
to work at the SEC for the past few years?

39
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:59,340
Obviously, there are a lot of great people there and I enjoy working there.

40
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:04,420
But from the perspective of crypto policy, it's been extremely frustrating because I

41
00:02:04,420 --> 00:02:07,380
feel like it's been one bad decision after another.

42
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:10,900
So yeah, I'm happy that we're in a new day.

43
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:12,100
You said you listened to the podcast.

44
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:15,740
You might have heard us say this before, but I'm kind of surprised that you're still there

45
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:18,860
given everything that you've had to push through at the SEC.

46
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:20,980
Do you ever consider leaving the agency?

47
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:21,980
Yeah.

48
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:25,620
I mean, I've been there for a long time and I thought that it would be good to have a

49
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:27,140
new voice come in there.

50
00:02:27,140 --> 00:02:29,440
So that certainly has crossed my mind.

51
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:35,140
The reason that I'm staying on for a little bit now is I really am looking forward to

52
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:36,460
Paul Atkins arriving.

53
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:37,840
I worked for him.

54
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:44,060
I know how he thinks and I think it will be good for the agency and good for capital markets.

55
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:48,860
In an ideal state, I'd be curious how the agency is really meant to function with regards

56
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:52,220
to dissents around different commissioners having different points of view.

57
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:53,820
Is that a collaborative process?

58
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:57,580
Does that happen in a vacuum and how should that work in principle?

59
00:02:57,580 --> 00:03:01,260
I mean, I think that it's a five-member commission for a reason.

60
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:06,340
You want to have different people with different perspectives on things and so it's natural

61
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:09,700
that you're going to get some dissents, concurrences and things like that.

62
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:16,020
When we do get along as people and as colleagues, we get along with each other so we can share

63
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:21,000
our thoughts about things and I'm a little predictable on some of these things.

64
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,840
So it's no surprise that I'm going to be dissenting on some of these things.

65
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:31,540
But I think that we could do a better job maybe learning from each other sometimes.

66
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:35,420
I think we all have something to learn from one another on these issues, but I really

67
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:39,900
felt, and I think you captured that when you said, what's it like to be there?

68
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:44,660
It really did sometimes feel like it was hard to get people to pay attention and that's

69
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:45,740
probably on me.

70
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:50,700
If I had done a better job making my points, maybe we would be in the position four years

71
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:56,060
ago of doing something proactive instead of just continuing year after year not to do

72
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:57,720
anything proactive.

73
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:01,440
When I had gotten started in this industry initially when I was working at Fidelity back

74
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:06,880
in 2014, it always struck me that there would be a fair amount of education necessary to

75
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:11,160
really talk to the regulators, explain how the technology works, explain some of the

76
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:12,840
product ideas.

77
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:17,480
And as that went forward, I started to notice that some of these regulators really start

78
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:18,480
to get it.

79
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:22,220
You being the leading one really with the initial safe harbor proposal.

80
00:04:22,220 --> 00:04:27,000
I'm sort of at a loss as to why we ended up in this situation in some respects with the

81
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,000
SEC.

82
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,500
And I get that question a lot.

83
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:35,140
Why did the agency try to prevent some of these companies from getting off the ground

84
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:38,860
and some of the intermediaries from actually accessing this market?

85
00:04:38,860 --> 00:04:39,860
Wondering if you can speak to that.

86
00:04:39,860 --> 00:04:42,620
I mean, I think it's kind of a multi-stranded thing.

87
00:04:42,620 --> 00:04:49,140
One is there was and is a lot of bad activity that is being perpetrated either in crypto

88
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:50,900
in the name of crypto.

89
00:04:50,900 --> 00:04:53,940
So that scares people and understandably so.

90
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:58,220
A regulator is going to look at that and say, why do I want that in my jurisdiction at all?

91
00:04:58,260 --> 00:05:00,140
I'd rather just shut that down.

92
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:04,100
And so I think it is hard to see through that bad activity.

93
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:07,620
And then there have been a lot of promises about all the great things that crypto was

94
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:08,620
going to do.

95
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:14,620
And many of those things, as with any new technology that's being tried and applied,

96
00:05:14,620 --> 00:05:15,760
it doesn't all work.

97
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:17,620
So of course, some things aren't going to work.

98
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:22,300
So if you see the bad conduct and you're not seeing these massive changes, I think that

99
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:26,620
leads to saying, well, whatever, we're just going to use the regulatory process to kind

100
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:27,980
of shut this down.

101
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:34,540
I was enthusiastic that Chair Gensler would come in and he had taught about this at MIT.

102
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:39,940
I was thinking, we're going to have someone who really does understand this and really

103
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:42,660
can figure out a good regulatory regime.

104
00:05:42,660 --> 00:05:46,860
He had built a regulatory regime for swaps when he was at the CFTC.

105
00:05:46,860 --> 00:05:51,540
I didn't love everything about that regime, but he had stood up a regime.

106
00:05:51,540 --> 00:05:55,700
In my experience, it didn't seem he found this technology so interesting.

107
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:00,340
Frankly, you guys talk a lot about Operation Chokepoint, and I think there are different

108
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:02,980
ways for Operation Chokepoint to manifest itself.

109
00:06:02,980 --> 00:06:10,220
And one way is you can just come in with regulations and you can make it really unattractive for

110
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:12,540
people to participate in the industry.

111
00:06:12,540 --> 00:06:17,300
You're always talking about Saab 121, or you were, which was helpful for me.

112
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:21,940
I thought it was a problem, but you and others really underscored for me that it was a problem.

113
00:06:21,940 --> 00:06:24,420
And that's a way of keeping legitimate actors out.

114
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:29,860
So I feel like it's a confluence of events that just led to this really negative spiral.

115
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:35,020
You hit on it and wouldn't be a Castle Island podcast without talking about Saab 121, but

116
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:38,140
when I really step back and reflect on the past few years, I totally agree with you on

117
00:06:38,140 --> 00:06:39,140
the bad behavior.

118
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:43,180
In a lot of ways, I was hoping that the SEC would be a little bit more active, or maybe

119
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:47,180
it's the DOJ cutting down on some of these unregulated offshore venues.

120
00:06:47,180 --> 00:06:49,700
I think that would have been healthier for the industry.

121
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:54,700
One thing for ICOs actually in the earlier days, some of the clearly unregulated securities

122
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:56,540
offerings that were happening.

123
00:06:56,540 --> 00:07:02,540
But when I look at the past two or three years, Saab 121 inclusive of this, it does feel like

124
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:06,100
there was a bigger force at play to just choke off the industry.

125
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:10,380
You have the debanking on the banking regulator side, we were seeing that left and right.

126
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:14,840
And then with respect to the SEC, if you can prevent the banks from coming in and custodying

127
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:19,720
these assets and being active in stable coins, that is just a great way to stymie innovation

128
00:07:19,720 --> 00:07:24,300
and prevent startups who want to sell into those institutions from getting off the ground.

129
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:29,200
Is it as easy as just the leadership at the SEC not wanting to do anything with the technology,

130
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:34,320
or is there a bigger political force here that was trying to shut this down?

131
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:35,320
I don't know.

132
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:37,120
We can speculate about that.

133
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:42,440
I think there's a lot of pressure to have a financial system that's very intermediated

134
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:47,840
because it's very easy to, easy is not the right word, but it's easier to regulate a

135
00:07:47,840 --> 00:07:52,880
financial system that has a lot of very large intermediaries because you know who to go

136
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:54,520
to when there's a problem.

137
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:55,800
So maybe there's some of that.

138
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:01,200
I think that a lot of people's motives, even people who are not favorable toward crypto

139
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:02,800
are good motives.

140
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:05,240
There may be bad motives too, I don't know.

141
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:08,600
I think the better thing to focus on is how can we change that?

142
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:13,400
It's not only something like SAB 121, but it's also how do we interact with investment

143
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:17,160
advisors who have some crypto in their portfolio?

144
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:22,120
How do we interact with auditors that are trying to figure out how to do proof of reserves?

145
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:26,840
There are lots of different ways that we interact with this, and so I think a lot of it was

146
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:27,840
negative.

147
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:29,160
I hope we can turn it positive.

148
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:35,140
I will just say in response to your wish that the SEC had been more active in some ways.

149
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:37,720
One is, and I'm always harping about this.

150
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:39,640
I think it's really important I say this.

151
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:41,760
We do have jurisdictional boundaries.

152
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:46,600
We're supposed to focus on activity in the US, and there are some instances when we're

153
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:52,600
looking at stuff outside that has an effect in the US, but really the goal is for us to

154
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:53,880
regulate here.

155
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:59,320
And so I think what I find so sad is that if we had set up a good regulatory regime

156
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:03,160
in the US, people probably wouldn't have done stuff outside the US.

157
00:09:03,160 --> 00:09:07,040
And if they had, people might have said, well, why aren't you in the US where there's a good

158
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:12,360
regulatory regime, and we can rely on the fact that there's some regulation there.

159
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:17,640
And so I think we pushed stuff out of the US that didn't end up helping American people,

160
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:19,440
and we wasted a lot of time.

161
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:24,400
And then with respect to ICOs, yes, I mean, some activity is securities activity, but

162
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:29,500
again, you've got to build some kind of a framework that works for people to do those

163
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:31,320
kinds of things.

164
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:35,520
And you might be able to do the initial offering through one of the existing exemptions.

165
00:09:35,560 --> 00:09:40,120
Because there was no long term way for getting those tokens out into the broader world, it

166
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:43,360
just, again, cultivates bad activity.

167
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:44,960
Yeah, I think that's spot on.

168
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:49,720
On your first point on the unregulated offshore venues, there was a time, it was a few years

169
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:53,840
ago now before they collapsed, but there was a time when I actually would say that FTX

170
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:57,200
was maybe our biggest miss, because when we had looked at some of the early rounds, we

171
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:03,440
said, well, this is an unregulated offshore venue clearly serving US citizens with

172
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:05,120
securities on the platform.

173
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:07,040
And they also run this market making firm.

174
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:08,920
This is clearly a conflict of interest.

175
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:13,200
There's going to be US alternatives here that pop up, but there didn't end up being that.

176
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:15,160
And so it ended up for a short period of time.

177
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:17,080
We're lucky we didn't invest in it, I suppose.

178
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:21,080
But it's interesting to think through just the deployment of capital and how that's been

179
00:10:21,080 --> 00:10:24,200
altered by the ambiguity in the regulatory climate.

180
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:25,840
Yeah, absolutely.

181
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:31,240
That's really scary for a regulator to see that, to see if you do your job badly, you

182
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:34,760
can contribute to some really bad things happening.

183
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:39,600
That's been a little bit the lesson of my career because I was around during, obviously,

184
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:42,360
the financial crisis of 2008.

185
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:45,080
And I think some of that stemmed, too, from bad regulation.

186
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:49,160
So it is definitely daunting when you think about that.

187
00:10:49,160 --> 00:10:53,320
One of the things I'd love to get your take on is just the role of Congress in defining

188
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:58,640
what a market structure should look like here versus the role of the SEC and maybe a collaborative

189
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:00,840
relationship with the CFTC.

190
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:05,560
How much can you do at the SEC here and where does Congress need to step in?

191
00:11:05,560 --> 00:11:10,080
I think we can do a lot initially just saying, here's what's within our jurisdiction, here's

192
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:11,080
what's outside.

193
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:14,640
And then if it's within, we can write some rules for that.

194
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:20,640
I do think at the end of the day, Congress will want, I mean, it does want to come in

195
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:25,360
and allocate jurisdiction for the stuff that I don't think is really covered by anyone

196
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:26,540
now.

197
00:11:26,540 --> 00:11:31,440
So if you have platforms that are trading things that are not securities and they're

198
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:34,660
not futures, where do they fit?

199
00:11:34,660 --> 00:11:38,780
That's where Congress is looking to try to figure out who that should go to.

200
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:39,780
Is it the CFTC?

201
00:11:39,780 --> 00:11:40,780
Is it us?

202
00:11:40,780 --> 00:11:46,820
In the interim, the CFTC and we can work together to help do some of the groundwork, help them

203
00:11:46,820 --> 00:11:53,060
understand what the landscape is and where regulatory authority might make the most sense.

204
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:55,840
But ultimately, obviously, that's going to be their call.

205
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:57,960
So I think we can work in parallel.

206
00:11:57,960 --> 00:12:02,800
Now that we are sort of taking a more proactive look, I think our technical assistance to

207
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:05,560
Congress can be more valuable as well.

208
00:12:05,560 --> 00:12:10,280
Once we understand what Congress wants to do, we can tell them whether this will fit

209
00:12:10,280 --> 00:12:13,280
with how we look at traditional securities markets.

210
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:16,200
And I think that can be helpful as I try to frame this out.

211
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:19,040
I mean, it's not an easy task for them.

212
00:12:19,040 --> 00:12:22,600
Definitely not an easy task, but it does seem like there's just an order of magnitude more

213
00:12:22,680 --> 00:12:27,160
engagement and knowledge in this current Congress than there was in the past one, particularly

214
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:28,760
in the financial services committees.

215
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:30,720
Yeah, I mean, there's a lot of knowledge.

216
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:34,320
I'm impressed that people who have so many other things to do have time to figure this

217
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:35,360
stuff out.

218
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:39,160
The other thing we have to be thinking about is we're looking at the industry as it is

219
00:12:39,160 --> 00:12:43,640
now, but we also want to make sure that whatever we build is going to work in the long term

220
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:49,360
because it's really hard to change legislation, hard to change statutes, hard to change regulations.

221
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:54,400
When I talk to institutional allocators that are deploying to funds, they always ask around

222
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:55,400
the use cases.

223
00:12:55,400 --> 00:13:00,080
So within crypto, you clearly have a store of value use case, Bitcoin being the leader

224
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:01,080
there.

225
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:05,600
You clearly have this open source operating system use case, the smart contract platforms,

226
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:07,880
Ethereum, Solana, Monad, whatnot.

227
00:13:07,880 --> 00:13:12,540
And then stable coins is just clearly a breakout killer app where I think it's 70% of every

228
00:13:12,540 --> 00:13:15,560
transaction on a blockchain is a US dollar moving.

229
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:20,480
International remittances is popping up as a great use case, but there are so many more

230
00:13:20,480 --> 00:13:25,360
use cases that could be there if there was regulatory clarity on how to bring one of

231
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:27,200
these things to market.

232
00:13:27,200 --> 00:13:32,160
And so many companies have not gotten started over the past four years because of that,

233
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:36,320
because of just the personal risk that the entrepreneur would need to take and all of

234
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:37,480
the brain damage.

235
00:13:37,480 --> 00:13:41,480
And I think in a lot of ways, these things would have to be some sort of a security out

236
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:43,280
of the gate to raise capital.

237
00:13:43,280 --> 00:13:46,680
And then the token itself, obviously, hopefully would not be at some point.

238
00:13:46,680 --> 00:13:51,240
But you were onto this really early with the safe harbor proposal, curious how your thinking

239
00:13:51,240 --> 00:13:55,360
has maybe changed over time or do you still think that could be something that we put

240
00:13:55,360 --> 00:13:56,360
into place?

241
00:13:56,360 --> 00:14:00,880
Yeah, I mean, I think something like that could work just to make sure that people have

242
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:07,960
a way to get the ecosystem working, get people using it without having the fear of the securities

243
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,360
law overhang, but also keeping a little pressure on people to actually keep doing something,

244
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:16,080
not just launch the tokens and then run away.

245
00:14:16,080 --> 00:14:20,960
I'm looking forward to what people have to say about an approach like that.

246
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:24,920
I think we can design something like that, certainly for the short term and then trying

247
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:29,520
to figure out if that's really the right long-term solution or if there's some better solution.

248
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:34,880
I think we do have to think about the complexity of if you have something where something is

249
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:41,240
going to morph from a security to a non-security, that is going to raise a lot of questions

250
00:14:41,240 --> 00:14:43,000
around where is the line?

251
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:44,800
When does that change happen?

252
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:49,640
You don't want to have to switch trading platforms because something has switched from a security

253
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:51,240
to a non-security.

254
00:14:51,240 --> 00:14:56,720
So I think if we focus on the fact that the token itself is probably often not the security.

255
00:14:56,720 --> 00:15:01,040
I mean, it may be sold as part of an investment contract and that's fine.

256
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:06,960
But the token itself, unless it has security-like characteristics, probably is not.

257
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:10,920
I think that might help us not have to draw those lines.

258
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:11,920
I don't know.

259
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:12,920
We'll see.

260
00:15:12,920 --> 00:15:16,000
I'm looking forward to just getting a lot of input on that point.

261
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:23,120
Unfortunately, we've really muddled the discussion by not being very legally precise by going

262
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:26,720
out there and saying, well, we think all of these tokens are securities.

263
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:27,960
I don't know that.

264
00:15:27,960 --> 00:15:30,880
I mean, I certainly don't look at it that way.

265
00:15:30,880 --> 00:15:35,720
It's unusual to have investment contracts that trade on secondary markets.

266
00:15:35,720 --> 00:15:38,640
This is all a little bit of a new territory for us.

267
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:42,840
Our interpretation of the Howey test has been very broad in my view.

268
00:15:42,840 --> 00:15:44,480
So we've got to figure it out.

269
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:48,440
But I think the safe harbor is a place that the discussion can at least start.

270
00:15:48,440 --> 00:15:50,680
Yeah, I think it was a great starting point.

271
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:54,660
You brought up disclosures in your answer there and that's something that was just totally

272
00:15:54,700 --> 00:15:59,580
eye-opening to me during the ICO phenomenon that you would dive into the actual network

273
00:15:59,580 --> 00:16:04,180
data and you'd look at the addresses of the core team or maybe some of the early investors

274
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:08,780
and you would see really bad behavior or you would see something in a white paper that

275
00:16:08,780 --> 00:16:11,180
was just not represented on chain.

276
00:16:11,180 --> 00:16:15,380
So I've always thought that it would be a great opportunity to build a disclosure framework.

277
00:16:15,380 --> 00:16:17,860
First of all, for the government maybe to have one.

278
00:16:17,860 --> 00:16:22,060
But I'm also curious if there's a role for private industry in pushing something like

279
00:16:22,060 --> 00:16:23,820
that as a standard.

280
00:16:23,820 --> 00:16:28,180
How do you think that could evolve in terms of just disclosing ownership, investing schedules

281
00:16:28,180 --> 00:16:29,660
and things like that?

282
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:30,980
I think that would be great.

283
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:33,980
I think it would benefit everyone to have that out there.

284
00:16:33,980 --> 00:16:39,080
And this is another example of where regulation has actually discouraged disclosure.

285
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:44,420
So now I think the industry may be more empowered to come up with some kind of standard.

286
00:16:44,420 --> 00:16:48,980
And we can bake that into regulation to the extent we think that it's evergreen, that

287
00:16:48,980 --> 00:16:50,340
it's not going to change a lot.

288
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:53,700
And I think some of the things that you raised are exactly the kind of thing I want to get

289
00:16:53,700 --> 00:16:54,700
to.

290
00:16:54,700 --> 00:16:58,860
I mean, people need to understand that the white paper actually is consistent with the

291
00:16:58,860 --> 00:16:59,860
code.

292
00:16:59,860 --> 00:17:04,260
They need to understand what the development team is thinking about.

293
00:17:04,260 --> 00:17:10,100
Is this really just an opportunity for them to very opportunistically dump a lot of tokens

294
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:13,460
on people and then just run and abandon things?

295
00:17:13,460 --> 00:17:20,300
I'm really excited to see now that we have a more positive regulatory approach to see

296
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:23,340
behavior being rewarded and bad behavior being punished.

297
00:17:23,340 --> 00:17:24,740
Yeah, me too.

298
00:17:24,740 --> 00:17:25,740
I don't know.

299
00:17:25,740 --> 00:17:29,580
Does that end up looking like an Edgar system for crypto assets or how do you think this

300
00:17:29,580 --> 00:17:30,940
could evolve?

301
00:17:30,940 --> 00:17:34,540
Maybe it's just telling people you've got to make these disclosures and they have to

302
00:17:34,540 --> 00:17:36,180
be available to the public.

303
00:17:36,180 --> 00:17:41,380
I don't know that we need to set up a formal Edgar system because again, whatever we build,

304
00:17:41,380 --> 00:17:45,500
it needs to recognize that a lot of these teams are very small and they're not going

305
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:47,140
to have lots of lawyers.

306
00:17:47,140 --> 00:17:49,660
They may not have any in-house lawyers.

307
00:17:49,660 --> 00:17:53,660
They may be relying on outside counsel and they can't pay a lot for that.

308
00:17:53,660 --> 00:17:57,020
So I want to make sure whatever it is, it's something that's doable.

309
00:17:57,020 --> 00:18:01,940
And I think we can probably look at how things are going in Europe to see how their disclosure

310
00:18:01,940 --> 00:18:05,580
system is working and whether that's helpful to us.

311
00:18:05,580 --> 00:18:06,580
Yeah, that's interesting.

312
00:18:06,580 --> 00:18:10,100
I think Mika certainly could be a model that could be explored.

313
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:15,460
Although I was looking at one of those disclosures the other day and they had a lot about scope

314
00:18:16,460 --> 00:18:19,180
greenhouse gas emissions and things like that.

315
00:18:19,180 --> 00:18:22,580
That's not really where I want to spend our disclosure efforts.

316
00:18:22,580 --> 00:18:26,700
Do you think the SEC will actually just focus on capital markets and investor protection?

317
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:27,700
Maybe that, yeah.

318
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:29,060
Yeah, that'd be nice.

319
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:32,740
You brought up the role of lawyers and it strikes me that the lawyers have been doing

320
00:18:32,740 --> 00:18:33,740
great in this market.

321
00:18:33,740 --> 00:18:38,140
I mean, there's just a lot of defense attorneys and probably the SEC has spent a fair bit

322
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:40,380
of money on some of these cases.

323
00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:44,940
So I guess just a general question around how should the agency really function when

324
00:18:44,940 --> 00:18:49,700
it comes to spending taxpayer dollars, prosecuting some of these things that are to say the least

325
00:18:49,700 --> 00:18:50,700
like not very clear.

326
00:18:50,700 --> 00:18:51,700
Yeah.

327
00:18:51,700 --> 00:18:57,020
I mean, I think that's the big question for us is how do we spend our regulatory resources?

328
00:18:57,020 --> 00:19:01,940
And it does matter which tool you use for what because litigation is very expensive

329
00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:02,940
for us.

330
00:19:02,940 --> 00:19:06,740
It is certainly expensive for people that we're litigating against.

331
00:19:06,740 --> 00:19:12,300
And so it's a tool we use, but we should use it judiciously and we shouldn't be using enforcement

332
00:19:12,380 --> 00:19:17,220
actions to try to write rules, which we were absolutely trying to do.

333
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:18,540
That's not good for anyone.

334
00:19:18,540 --> 00:19:19,620
It's expensive.

335
00:19:19,620 --> 00:19:24,320
You've got people at the SEC who are experts in policy and they're not at the table when

336
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:27,540
you're having an enforcement settlement negotiation with someone.

337
00:19:27,540 --> 00:19:31,500
They may be consulted, but they're not driving that negotiation.

338
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:32,500
And same on the outside.

339
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:35,580
I mean, you hire litigators to litigate these cases.

340
00:19:35,580 --> 00:19:39,380
You're not hiring a regulatory lawyer to do this.

341
00:19:39,380 --> 00:19:46,340
So it's just been a really bad use of resources and as a result, we haven't had as many resources

342
00:19:46,340 --> 00:19:48,380
to go after the truly bad actors.

343
00:19:48,380 --> 00:19:53,440
Now some of that stuff again may not be within our jurisdiction, but we can be on the lookout

344
00:19:53,440 --> 00:19:54,440
for it.

345
00:19:54,440 --> 00:19:56,780
We can be referring it to other regulators.

346
00:19:56,780 --> 00:20:03,060
I think if we get to a better place regulatorily, we'll have more enforcement resources to use

347
00:20:03,060 --> 00:20:04,620
in better ways.

348
00:20:04,620 --> 00:20:05,620
That makes total sense.

349
00:20:06,020 --> 00:20:09,580
One of the areas that I've been interested in for a long time, but we haven't deployed

350
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:13,340
a lot of capital against is this idea of tokenized securities.

351
00:20:13,340 --> 00:20:18,820
So things that maybe exist in the real world or net new instruments that are online, but

352
00:20:18,820 --> 00:20:20,060
they're actually securities.

353
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:21,740
They want to be securities.

354
00:20:21,740 --> 00:20:25,180
And it's been hard for us from an investment perspective because we see a lot of really

355
00:20:25,180 --> 00:20:29,660
good ideas, but there are clear regulatory impediments to some of these things working

356
00:20:29,660 --> 00:20:33,660
in terms of secondary markets and the definition of custody.

357
00:20:33,660 --> 00:20:34,780
How are you thinking about that?

358
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:39,100
And I know that you've put out a really great piece around the journey begins, which is

359
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:40,100
February 4th.

360
00:20:40,100 --> 00:20:42,540
You put up this statement and it does touch on this.

361
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:44,780
So I'm curious how you're thinking about that category.

362
00:20:44,780 --> 00:20:49,980
Yeah, I mean, I think it will be an area that we see a lot of people trying to innovate

363
00:20:49,980 --> 00:20:51,180
and experiment.

364
00:20:51,180 --> 00:20:56,900
I think it hasn't really happened very much because anything that had any kind of mention

365
00:20:56,900 --> 00:21:01,620
of tokenization or any kind of mention of blockchain, I think people were scared to

366
00:21:01,620 --> 00:21:04,260
engage with us about that.

367
00:21:04,260 --> 00:21:07,180
There's been some experimentation and I think that's great.

368
00:21:07,180 --> 00:21:09,380
We've seen money market funds tokenized.

369
00:21:09,380 --> 00:21:13,820
I think people have started to step into it, but I expect we'll see a lot more of that.

370
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:14,820
I don't know where it will go.

371
00:21:14,820 --> 00:21:17,620
I think it's exciting to think about the possibilities.

372
00:21:17,620 --> 00:21:22,820
You can take a bond and if you can tokenize it, you can make sure that all the data travels

373
00:21:22,820 --> 00:21:26,940
with that bond instead of having a lot of documents on the side.

374
00:21:26,940 --> 00:21:31,700
I think that it could be more efficient in a lot of areas, but I'm not the technologist.

375
00:21:31,700 --> 00:21:35,420
I'll leave that to you guys to figure out what's going to work and what's not, but I

376
00:21:35,420 --> 00:21:40,380
think it certainly will see some more movement in the U.S. on that.

377
00:21:40,380 --> 00:21:44,380
One of the things I wrestle with as a venture investor with this category is let's say that

378
00:21:44,380 --> 00:21:47,860
there was a framework in place and hopefully there is soon.

379
00:21:47,860 --> 00:21:52,340
All of the centralized institutions can do this type of activity, tokenized securities,

380
00:21:52,340 --> 00:21:53,340
moving it around.

381
00:21:53,340 --> 00:21:57,700
I completely understand how that could reduce cost at some of these institutions.

382
00:21:57,700 --> 00:22:00,380
There'd be potentially net new financial instruments.

383
00:22:00,820 --> 00:22:06,140
It's not obvious to me what the venture scale startup opportunities are necessarily in some

384
00:22:06,140 --> 00:22:11,460
of those categories, but if you could actually take some of these securities and interface

385
00:22:11,460 --> 00:22:16,280
with public blockchains in a DeFi context, I think that could get really interesting.

386
00:22:16,280 --> 00:22:20,300
Do you think that's a bridge too far to figure that part out right away or could that be

387
00:22:20,300 --> 00:22:21,300
part of this?

388
00:22:21,300 --> 00:22:23,260
I hope people will be thinking about that.

389
00:22:23,260 --> 00:22:27,880
You guys talk all the time about public versus permissioned blockchains.

390
00:22:27,920 --> 00:22:31,960
The benefit comes from having a public blockchain and figuring out what you can build on top

391
00:22:31,960 --> 00:22:32,960
of it.

392
00:22:32,960 --> 00:22:37,720
So I think that's what I'm excited to see people experimenting with.

393
00:22:37,720 --> 00:22:42,240
And there'll be a lot of need to educate us regulators on a lot of things.

394
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:46,600
I think this is going to be an area where regulators will have to be able to get comfortable

395
00:22:46,600 --> 00:22:47,600
with this stuff.

396
00:22:47,600 --> 00:22:52,960
But I think that for me is where I think a lot of the really interesting innovation will

397
00:22:52,960 --> 00:22:53,960
happen.

398
00:22:53,960 --> 00:22:58,560
We might look more to people who actually are thinking about this all the time and investing,

399
00:22:58,560 --> 00:23:02,720
putting their money where their mouth is to see what really is going to take off and what

400
00:23:02,720 --> 00:23:03,720
won't.

401
00:23:03,720 --> 00:23:07,080
Yeah, it's instructive that at least stable coins work in this context.

402
00:23:07,080 --> 00:23:09,640
And I would argue a lot of those are not securities.

403
00:23:09,640 --> 00:23:11,400
Maybe some of them end up being who knows.

404
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:15,980
But just to show what you can do with a US dollar and to extend that idea to other types

405
00:23:15,980 --> 00:23:19,440
of assets, I think that's the design space that a lot of entrepreneurs would like to

406
00:23:19,440 --> 00:23:20,720
explore.

407
00:23:20,960 --> 00:23:25,120
Good first step would be getting the centralized secondary trading venues up and running.

408
00:23:25,120 --> 00:23:28,980
But I think the next question for a lot of people is, well, what else can I do with this?

409
00:23:28,980 --> 00:23:31,960
Can I have this as a collateral asset in a DeFi pool?

410
00:23:31,960 --> 00:23:34,520
It'll be fun to watch what happens.

411
00:23:34,520 --> 00:23:40,160
Just flipping this back towards my side, what should we be thinking about as investors in

412
00:23:40,160 --> 00:23:41,160
this space?

413
00:23:41,160 --> 00:23:42,880
And what should we be advising entrepreneurs?

414
00:23:42,880 --> 00:23:47,320
There was a time when Come In and Register was offered to us, and it turns out that was

415
00:23:47,320 --> 00:23:48,920
a really, really bad idea.

416
00:23:48,920 --> 00:23:50,280
Yeah, I'm sorry.

417
00:23:51,040 --> 00:23:52,980
What should we be telling our entrepreneurs?

418
00:23:52,980 --> 00:23:57,920
We really do need people to come in and talk to us and try to figure out what registration

419
00:23:57,920 --> 00:24:02,120
would look like if that's the right thing, or what can we carve out that clearly isn't

420
00:24:02,120 --> 00:24:03,120
within our space?

421
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:07,800
And how can we provide clarity to people so that they can be comfortable?

422
00:24:07,800 --> 00:24:11,840
Because one of the things I've seen over the past several years is that people have said

423
00:24:11,840 --> 00:24:15,760
to me, hey, Hester, there's something that I would like to do, but I'm concerned that

424
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:19,280
you might come back five years later and say there's a securities angle.

425
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:21,760
I actually don't think there is, but you might tell me there is.

426
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:23,880
And so people have held off.

427
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:29,000
I guess it would be helpful for me, for people to come in and say, here are areas that would

428
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:33,080
be helpful for you to make clear are not within your jurisdiction.

429
00:24:33,080 --> 00:24:37,920
And then if people have ideas about what is a good disclosure regime look like?

430
00:24:37,920 --> 00:24:40,120
What should the broker-dealer regime look like?

431
00:24:40,120 --> 00:24:44,560
Do you want to have a special purpose broker-dealer framework, or do you want to throw that out

432
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:47,280
and just work with the existing broker-dealer framework?

433
00:24:47,280 --> 00:24:50,080
What does custody look like for investment advisors?

434
00:24:50,080 --> 00:24:52,120
How should we be thinking about that issue?

435
00:24:52,120 --> 00:24:53,840
So I do want people to talk to us.

436
00:24:53,840 --> 00:24:59,320
If people have particular projects that they're trying to do and they want no action relief,

437
00:24:59,320 --> 00:25:01,360
our doors are open there as well.

438
00:25:01,360 --> 00:25:02,360
People need to be patient.

439
00:25:02,360 --> 00:25:06,440
It's going to take us a while to sort through all this, but we really do want to hear from

440
00:25:06,440 --> 00:25:07,440
people.

441
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:11,760
I know that is going to take a while because people really feel they don't trust that they

442
00:25:11,760 --> 00:25:15,680
can really come in and talk to us, but I hope that gradually people will realize they can

443
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:16,680
do that.

444
00:25:16,680 --> 00:25:17,680
Yeah.

445
00:25:17,680 --> 00:25:21,600
Well, I think the fact that you've stuck around and Commissioner Ueda really seems to be attuned

446
00:25:21,600 --> 00:25:27,320
to these same issues gives people a lot of comfort that it's not a trap this time.

447
00:25:27,320 --> 00:25:28,960
You're not going to step on our mind.

448
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:33,240
I mean, I think that's part of the reason we need to move fast and try to get things

449
00:25:33,240 --> 00:25:38,080
in place so that people can really trust that this is going to last also.

450
00:25:38,080 --> 00:25:40,960
And Congress will be very helpful in that as well.

451
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:45,040
Is there anything the industry is doing now that you would advise us to try to stop?

452
00:25:45,160 --> 00:25:48,120
I mean, there's certainly some bad behavior going out there.

453
00:25:48,120 --> 00:25:50,840
I worry about the self-inflicted wounds more than anything.

454
00:25:50,840 --> 00:25:55,160
This industry has shown an amazing propensity to just get in its own way over the years.

455
00:25:55,160 --> 00:25:56,640
Yeah, there are a couple things.

456
00:25:56,640 --> 00:25:59,360
One, I would say don't commit fraud.

457
00:25:59,360 --> 00:26:00,360
Good start.

458
00:26:00,360 --> 00:26:06,000
Second, I would say remember the lessons from the traditional financial world.

459
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,980
You don't get a pass on over leverage.

460
00:26:08,980 --> 00:26:13,200
You've got to think about what bad things could happen and prepare for those.

461
00:26:13,360 --> 00:26:17,080
In this world, sometimes those things can happen much faster because it's just code

462
00:26:17,080 --> 00:26:18,080
working.

463
00:26:18,080 --> 00:26:22,200
If you do something, you can have a really negative consequence very quickly.

464
00:26:22,200 --> 00:26:23,400
So prepare for that.

465
00:26:23,400 --> 00:26:24,400
Think about that.

466
00:26:24,400 --> 00:26:26,480
But I mean, that's stuff that you guys all know.

467
00:26:26,480 --> 00:26:32,040
I think in terms of interacting with the SEC, remember that we've got limited resources

468
00:26:32,040 --> 00:26:33,840
and that we're trying to work through things.

469
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:38,520
So when you bring something into us, something like you're trying to get an ETP through,

470
00:26:38,520 --> 00:26:42,680
remember that having it as buttoned down as possible is going to be helpful.

471
00:26:42,720 --> 00:26:47,480
I know everyone's trying to get in line first, but you have to think about the fact that

472
00:26:47,480 --> 00:26:51,240
if we've got to look through your filing and their technical problems with it, that's time

473
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:55,120
taken away from working on a filing that actually is ready to go.

474
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:58,640
And then my other piece of advice is just really basic.

475
00:26:58,640 --> 00:26:59,800
We iterate.

476
00:26:59,800 --> 00:27:02,560
That's how we have worked with traditional financial markets.

477
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:06,960
So if you can say, okay, look, Hester, here's something that you guys did in the traditional

478
00:27:06,960 --> 00:27:07,960
financial markets.

479
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:11,280
We're trying to take it one step further or we're trying to apply that same principle

480
00:27:11,320 --> 00:27:15,000
here in these markets, that's helpful to us.

481
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:18,560
We can hang our hat on what we did before and then we can say, look, what we're doing

482
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:21,240
now is just an extension of that.

483
00:27:21,240 --> 00:27:24,280
And then being willing to have that conversation with us.

484
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:29,360
If we ask questions, come back with answers and be willing to say, okay, SEC, if you're

485
00:27:29,360 --> 00:27:31,880
not wanting to move forward, explain to us why not.

486
00:27:31,880 --> 00:27:33,400
What's the sticking point?

487
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:38,320
Be willing to press us when we say no, to understand why we're saying no.

488
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:40,880
That can get us further faster.

489
00:27:41,040 --> 00:27:42,720
That makes total sense.

490
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:45,640
So from where I sit, it feels like kind of a new day.

491
00:27:45,640 --> 00:27:50,320
I am seeing investment firms that are starting to mobilize around, okay, maybe we'll move

492
00:27:50,320 --> 00:27:52,120
from an ERA to an RIA.

493
00:27:52,120 --> 00:27:54,240
We're going to expand our strategy.

494
00:27:54,240 --> 00:27:56,840
Certainly seeing just a lot more startups get off the ground.

495
00:27:56,840 --> 00:28:02,800
It's been very, very busy since November over here and a lot of interesting categories that

496
00:28:02,800 --> 00:28:07,080
you wouldn't necessarily have seen entrepreneur quality as high a couple of years ago.

497
00:28:07,080 --> 00:28:10,880
So people leaving big tech firms to start ambitious projects.

498
00:28:10,880 --> 00:28:14,800
Do you feel that energy, is that visible to the SEC at all, that this is actually already

499
00:28:14,800 --> 00:28:16,280
having a positive effect?

500
00:28:16,280 --> 00:28:19,080
Yeah, I think I'm seeing glimmers of that.

501
00:28:19,080 --> 00:28:21,920
That is exactly what regulatory clarity can do.

502
00:28:21,920 --> 00:28:28,240
Because if you're asking someone to leave a comfortable job at an established company

503
00:28:28,240 --> 00:28:33,040
to start a venture only five years later to have the government basically shut that venture

504
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:37,960
down and take away everything that you've been working on, people aren't going to do it.

505
00:28:37,960 --> 00:28:42,720
But if people know, okay, I know I'm going to run into some regulatory obstacles, but

506
00:28:42,720 --> 00:28:46,840
I'm going to have a regulator that I can work through those obstacles with, it's worth taking

507
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:48,320
that risk.

508
00:28:48,320 --> 00:28:52,520
And that has always been the kind of country the US has been, is that we've been a country

509
00:28:52,520 --> 00:28:58,120
that's rewarded people taking those risks, being willing to step away from a comfortable

510
00:28:58,120 --> 00:29:01,440
career into something new to try something new.

511
00:29:02,080 --> 00:29:05,240
We need to be the best country in the world to do that.

512
00:29:05,240 --> 00:29:09,680
So I hope that in this little area where I'm working, I can help contribute to that.

513
00:29:09,680 --> 00:29:12,040
Well, I think that's a great place to leave it.

514
00:29:12,040 --> 00:29:15,960
I guess I'll close by just saying thank you for all that you've done to bring some of

515
00:29:15,960 --> 00:29:17,140
these issues to light.

516
00:29:17,140 --> 00:29:21,840
It's often felt like no one was really understanding the plight of some of these entrepreneurs.

517
00:29:21,840 --> 00:29:24,160
So really appreciate you hanging in there.

518
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:25,520
Matt, I appreciate it.

519
00:29:25,520 --> 00:29:30,920
And I appreciate what you all do to try to help us regulators and others understand what's

520
00:29:30,920 --> 00:29:32,240
going on in the industry.

521
00:29:32,240 --> 00:29:33,240
Well, thanks so much.

522
00:29:33,240 --> 00:29:35,040
We'd love to have you on again soon.

523
00:29:35,040 --> 00:29:36,040
Sounds good.

524
00:29:36,040 --> 00:29:37,040
Take care.

525
